me{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-\\infty }\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-1)$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d51ea8-9598-589f-91db-00b4a3099381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60d51ea8-9598-589f-91db-00b4a3099381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304538} :answer-field/id #uuid "42791177-a301-5ca8-9f3d-016ee9405d10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ae9b26e-4b4e-5f6f-aa03-3c0a8cc95e5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-302689" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2))=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039967/assets/a7e36a5c534a63199ea97985f1301db0c7675adc8fa0d17f33735c61eec9e871.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-2)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f26a533-08dc-5210-81c6-a68c05b09c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304541} {:answer/id #uuid "c78c4d96-d939-5113-916a-53754e85989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a533-08dc-5210-81c6-a68c05b09c0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304541} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f5a72f-b190-5748-931c-04aa6c33acba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "301cfe3d-cf46-5095-bff2-984d0168d45b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339380" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039967/assets/25cea33f2fc3a3bcdbc10ca03f93bd6059eb7d0a7dfcda5eb59f6f671387aea3.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Moreover, from the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x+5))=2\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70a86eac-72b3-53a4-ab40-babc50728e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a86eac-72b3-53a4-ab40-babc50728e2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304545} :answer-field/id #uuid "c657a2d2-7ac9-5c68-bb7b-c3788dff7152" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "54e014fa-7fcf-5f0c-af34-9e133e83876b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339477" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039967/assets/24f07da42a537ded7916fc2440dcf3ee1ccdfc81c7b027b47e97814fec059f5a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff642659-1354-519e-bf4c-124d5941f278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff642659-1354-519e-bf4c-124d5941f278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304548} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e4bd1d-929f-5865-81dd-f24257086327" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5e4c600-f08d-56a5-b0e5-8154dc0b42bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-339490" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039967/assets/68f1ebd0457e0c127f8ed6c979feba58196aa6bb43d97d211efa9b39bc33f637.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $x=\\sqrt{3}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}}sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
